\title{Other connectives and logical equivalence}
\date{2026-02-11T14:18:49Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Remark}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{What about #{\neg P} and #{P \iff Q}?  They are
logically equivalent to other, more primitive things:
\ul{
  \li{#{\neg P \equiv P \to \False}}
  \li{#{P \iff Q \equiv (P \to Q) \land (Q \to P)}}
}
That is, to prove #{\neg P}, we can suppose #{P} is true and prove \False; and to prove #{P \iff Q}, we proceed by proving both #{P \to Q} and #{Q \to P}.
}

\p{In general, if you are trying to prove #{P} and #{P \equiv Q}, it is always OK to prove #{Q} instead of #{P}.  Sometimes it is easier to prove a proposition in one form rather than another, even when the two are logically equivalent.  For example:
\ul{
  \li{When proving #{P \to Q}, sometimes we can prove the contrapositive #{\neg Q \to \neg P} instead.}
  \li{When proving a negation of a complex proposition, it often makes sense to first simplify it using de Morgan laws.  For example, to prove #{\neg (P \lor Q)}, it can make sense to prove #{\neg P \land \neg Q} instead.}
}
}
